Part 4 – Reflection

Although applications can perform business logic, enterprise systems still place some logic inside the database
to improve security, consistency, automation, and performance.

##Views
help protect sensitive information by allowing users to access only the data they need without exposing underlying tables.
This improves data security and simplifies reporting.

##Procedures
centralize business logic inside the database. Instead of repeating the same code in multiple applications,
a procedure can be reused and maintained in one location.

##Triggers
automatically respond to database events such as inserts, updates, or deletes.
They are commonly used for auditing and enforcing business rules without requiring user intervention.

##Scheduler
Jobs automate routine tasks such as report generation, backups, and maintenance activities.
This reduces manual work and ensures important processes run on time.

In conclusion, enterprise systems use database objects because they provide secure access, reusable business logic,
automatic auditing, and scheduled automation. This makes the system easier to manage, maintain,
and control across multiple applications and users.